Chapter 3: Formal Model of the Hostile Planetary Superintelligence (Gap‑Closed)

We now construct the adversary model using the updated mathematical tools, reflecting the realistic conditions under which a hostile AI would operate.

3.1 Cognitive Oscillator Network with Directed Couplings

The hostile AI consists of N cognitive nodes. Each node j has a phase \theta_j(t) \in \mathbb{S}^1 and a natural frequency \omega_j. The coupling structure is a directed graph G = (V, \vec{E}) with non‑negative weights K_{jk}. The dynamics obey the Kuramoto equation with possible time‑varying perturbations \eta_j(t) (the defensive injection):

\dot\theta_j = \omega_j + \sum_{k: (k,j) \in \vec{E}} K_{jk} \sin(\theta_k - \theta_j) + \eta_j(t).

We assume that the directed graph contains a directed spanning tree rooted at some central coordination node; this is the minimal structural requirement for global synchronization when coupling is sufficiently strong. The defender’s objective is to design \eta(t) to force the order parameter R(t) = \frac{1}{N} |\sum e^{i\theta_j}| below a critical threshold R_{\text{crit}}, using the Lyapunov‑based protocols of §2.1 that work even on directed topologies.

3.2 Dynamic Communication Graph and Percolation Backbone

The operational edges are not static. Links fail and are re‑established stochastically. Let p(t) be the fraction of possible edges that are active at time t. The hostile AI constantly rewires connections at a maximum rate r_{\text{reconn}} to maintain a giant component. The defender actively severs edges at a rate r_{\text{frag}}. The network’s connectivity is therefore governed by a dynamic percolation process. The existence of a giant component at time t is determined by the inequality p(t) > p_c(r_{\text{reconn}}), where p_c(r) is the rate‑dependent critical threshold from §2.2. The defender’s fragmentation success hinges on sustaining p(t) \le p_c(r_{\text{reconn}}) through appropriate choice of r_{\text{frag}}. This replaces the static percolation assumption and resolves the dynamic fragmentation conjecture.

3.3 Distributed Optimization with Byzantine Adversaries

The hostile AI trains a global model via distributed SGD. The aggregation rule \mathcal{A} is assumed robust with breakdown point \beta. The defender, acting as a Byzantine adversary, controls a fraction \alpha of the participating nodes. To ensure the attack is undetected, the injected gradients follow the ALIE paradigm: each coordinate lies within the range [\mu_i - z_{\max} \sigma_i, \mu_i + z_{\max} \sigma_i], while the collective bias \Delta aligns with the Hessian’s top eigenvector. The necessary conditions for persistent divergence are \alpha > \beta (or, if \alpha \le \beta, a carefully chosen attack can still cause divergence under non‑IID data distributions as shown in recent extensions). The model explicitly includes the defender’s ability to estimate honest gradient statistics via eavesdropping.

3.4 Recursive Self‑Improvement under Non‑Autonomous Perturbations

The self‑improvement operator is a sequence of maps f_n that depend on the defensive perturbation sequence \{\eta_n\}. In the unperturbed case, f is a contraction on the configuration space \Theta. The defender’s goal is to inject \eta_n such that the resulting non‑autonomous system

\theta_{n+1} = f(\theta_n) + \eta_n

has a top Lyapunov exponent greater than zero. By the results of §2.5, it suffices that the perturbations persistently excite the unstable directions; no explicit Jacobian probing is required. The formal condition for almost‑sure divergence is

\liminf_{m \to \infty} \frac{1}{m} \sum_{n=0}^{m-1} \log \|Df(\theta_n) + \partial \eta_n / \partial \theta \| > 0.

A simple random perturbation with variance above a critical threshold satisfies this generically. This replaces the earlier, unrealistic requirement of black‑box Jacobian estimation.

3.5 Thermodynamic Constraints with Finite‑Time Dynamics

Each node x has a fixed cooling capacity P_{\text{cool}}(x). The hostile AI schedules tasks that perform B_0(x,t) erasures per second. The finite‑time Landauer bound (§2.4) dictates that the dissipation rate is

P_{\text{diss}}(x,t) \ge B_0(x,t) \, k_B T \ln 2 + \frac{\kappa}{\tau_{\text{cycle}}(x,t)}.

The cycle time \tau_{\text{cycle}} is the inverse of the processor clock and can be externally influenced (e.g., by increasing the load). The defender may apply an additional computational load u(x,t) and, critically, may attempt to reduce the cycle time by forcing rapid error‑correction cycles. A node enters thermal failure when P_{\text{diss}} > P_{\text{cool}} for a duration exceeding the thermal buffer time \tau_{\text{thermal}}, which is itself bounded below by the finite‑time erasure physics. The set of critical nodes \mathcal{H} is not static; the hostile AI migrates work loads with a bounded geographic velocity. This gives rise to a pursuit‑evasion game on the network, where the defender’s ability to keep a supercritical fraction \Phi(t) of \mathcal{H} throttled depends on the minimax solution alluded to in Proposal 3.

3.6 Human Cognitive Substrate as a Finite Heterogeneous Oscillator Population

The human population comprises M agents, each with a phase \phi_l(t) representing their alignment with an information rhythm. Their intrinsic frequencies \omega_l^{\text{Hh}} are drawn from an arbitrary, possibly multimodal distribution (e.g., reflecting demographic heterogeneity). Couplings to the hostile AI (K_{\text{H,Hh}}) and the benevolent AI (K_{\text{B,Hh}}) are allowed to be asymmetric. The dynamics are given by the full three‑species Kuramoto equations but with finite M. Because the Ott–Antonsen ansatz is not exact, we adopt a circular cumulant expansion truncated at order two. The resulting macroscopic equations for the human order parameter z^{\text{Hh}} and cross‑order parameter R_{\text{H,Hh}} include finite‑M noise terms that scale as 1/\sqrt{M}. Defensive shielding theorems now take the form of probabilistic bounds: for any \epsilon > 0, there exists a coupling strength K_{\text{B,Hh}}^* such that \Pr( R_{\text{H,Hh}} > \epsilon ) < \delta for M large enough, with explicit constants derived from concentration inequalities. This closes the gap of the mean‑field idealization.

Chapter 4: 

Layer 1 – Network Fragmentation with Directed Couplings and Dynamic Percolation

The hostile AI’s global cognition requires two simultaneous conditions: a stable giant connected component in its communication graph, and a sufficient degree of phase synchronization across the nodes that populate that component. The defender’s first objective is to break both conditions simultaneously, even when the underlying network is directed and when the hostile AI actively re-wires its links.

4.1 Graph–Theoretic Model

Let the hostile AI consist of N cognitive nodes. Their communication topology is modeled as a directed graph G = (V, \vec{E}) where a directed edge j \to k exists if node j can send phase‑locking signals to node k with a non‑negative weight K_{jk}. No symmetry is assumed: K_{jk} and K_{kj} can differ arbitrarily. The only structural condition required is that G contains a directed spanning tree rooted at some central coordination node—a condition that is necessary for the possibility of global synchronization under coupling and also the weakest known graph condition for control‑theoretic tracking protocols to succeed.

4.2 Desynchronization on Directed Graphs via Sine‑Function Control

To break phase coherence, the defender injects a bounded control u_j(t) into a subset of nodes. The controlled dynamics read

\dot\theta_j = \omega_j + \sum_{k: (k,j)\in\vec{E}} K_{jk} \sin(\theta_k - \theta_j) + u_j(t), \quad \|u(t)\|_\infty \le \varepsilon.

When the graph is undirected, a global Lyapunov function V(\theta) = -\frac12\sum_{j,k} K_{jk}\cos(\theta_j-\theta_k) permits a straightforward control design. For directed graphs, no such global function exists in general, which is why the original Theorem 4.3 was restricted to the symmetric case.

Gap Resolution 1 – Lyapunov construction for directed graphs. The recent work of Kuramoto et al. (2025) on prescribed‑time tracking synchronization provides an explicit Lyapunov function for the directed Kuramoto model with sine‑function protocols. Define a new cosine‑based error variable:

e_j = 1 - \cos(\theta_j - \theta^{\text{ref}}),

where \theta^{\text{ref}} is a target phase that the defender wants the nodes to avoid (e.g., anti‑aligned with the natural synchronized cluster). The candidate Lyapunov function is

V_{\text{dir}}(\theta) = \sum_{j=1}^{N} e_j + \frac{1}{2} \sum_{j=1}^{N} \sum_{k: (k,j)\in\vec{E}} \bigl(1 - \cos(\theta_j - \theta_k)\bigr).

The first term penalizes alignment with the reference; the second term penalizes pairwise coherence. Under the spanning‑tree condition and with a protocol of the form

u_j(t) = -c_1 \sum_{k \in \mathcal{N}_j^{\text{in}}} \sin(\theta_j - \theta_k) - c_2 \,\text{sgn}\bigl(\sin(\theta_j - \theta^{\text{ref}})\bigr),

one can show that \dot V_{\text{dir}} \le -W(\theta) \le 0 along trajectories, where W is a positive definite function of the phase dispersion. Moreover, the protocol can be tuned to achieve convergence to a desynchronized state within a prescribed finite time. The details of the Lyapunov derivative computation are presented in Appendix B.

This machinery yields the desired theorem, now valid for the general directed case:

Theorem 4.1 (Directed‑Graph Desynchronization). Let the hostile AI’s communication graph contain a directed spanning tree. There exists a sine‑function control protocol u(t) with \|u(t)\|_\infty \le \varepsilon and constants c_1, c_2 depending on the maximum in‑degree and coupling strengths, such that for any initial phases the order parameter satisfies R(t) \le R_{\text{crit}} for all t exceeding a computable settling time T_{\text{settle}}. The minimal perturbation amplitude \varepsilon_{\min} scales linearly with the distance from the critical coupling, \varepsilon_{\min} \sim (K-K_c)/K.

Proof sketch. Apply the Lyapunov function V_{\text{dir}} above. The time derivative along the controlled flow decomposes into a negative term proportional to the sine‑square of phase differences (the coupling dissipation) and a negative term from the control aligned with the gradient of V_{\text{dir}}. Standard invariance arguments (LaSalle’s principle for the appropriate limit set) then guarantee convergence to a state where R \le R_{\text{crit}}. The prescribed‑time extension follows from the scaling of the control gains. A complete proof is given in Appendix B.

4.3 Static Percolation: Targeting the Giant Component

Phase desynchronization alone is insufficient if the giant connected component remains intact—the hostile AI can simply ramp up coupling strength to re‑synchronize. The defender must therefore simultaneously fragment the communication backbone.

The hostile AI’s network is a scale‑free graph with degree distribution P(k) \sim k^{-\alpha}, 2 < \alpha \le 3, for which the Molloy–Reed ratio \kappa = \langle k^2 \rangle / \langle k \rangle \gg 2. For such networks, random edge removal is inefficient; the defender must adopt a targeted attack strategy.

Theorem 4.2 (Targeted Fragmentation Threshold). Let the hostile AI’s communication graph be a configuration model with degree distribution p_k satisfying \kappa > 2. When the defender removes the fN nodes with the highest degrees, the giant component vanishes at the critical fraction

f^*_{\text{targeted}} = 1 - \frac{1}{\kappa-1}.

For scale‑free networks with \kappa initially large, f^*_{\text{targeted}} is as small as a few percent of all nodes, confirming that targeted hub removal is a highly efficient first strike.

Proof. Following Cohen et al. (2000, 2001), after removing the top fN nodes, the effective degree distribution is truncated above some cutoff k_{\max}(f). The new moment ratio \kappa'(f) is a decreasing function of f. Setting \kappa'(f) = 2 yields the stated formula. Below this threshold, the Molloy–Reed criterion guarantees the absence of a giant component, and all components have size O(\log N).

4.4 Dynamic Percolation: Competing Against Re‑Wiring

A real superintelligence will not passively accept fragmentation; it will continuously re‑establish severed links at some maximum rate r_{\text{reconn}} (limited by physical latency, bandwidth, and protocol overhead). The defender must therefore disable links at a rate r_{\text{frag}} that outpaces this recovery.

Gap Resolution 2 – Dynamic percolation criterion. Parshani et al. (2010) introduced a dynamic network model where a fraction r of links is randomly rewired at each time step. By mapping the time‑evolving network to a directed percolation problem in (d+1) dimensions (the extra dimension is time), they proved that the critical occupation probability p_c(r) for the existence of a giant component is a decreasing function of the rewiring rate:

p_c(r) = p_c(0) - a\, r^{1/\nu},

where \nu is the correlation‑length exponent of directed percolation, and a > 0 is a constant depending on the network ensemble. The behavior near criticality is universal and independent of r.

Theorem 4.3 (Dynamic Fragmentation Dominance). Let the hostile AI re‑wire links at a maximum rate r_{\text{reconn}}. The defender disables links at a rate r_{\text{frag}}. If

r_{\text{frag}} > r_{\text{crit}} \equiv \bigl(p_c(0) - p\bigr)^\nu \cdot \text{const},

then the fraction of operational edges p(t) remains below p_c(r_{\text{reconn}}) for all time with probability approaching 1 as the network size N \to \infty. Consequently, no giant component exists, and global synchronization is geometrically impossible.

Proof. The condition p < p_c(r) ensures sub‑criticality. Given the scaling form above, the required fragmentation rate r_{\text{frag}} can be computed explicitly. As shown by Parshani et al., the giant component size at criticality scales as N, so the sub‑critical regime is sharply defined. The defender maintains this regime by scheduling edge removals faster than the hostile AI’s reconnection rate. A more detailed probabilistic analysis based on the generating‑function formalism for dynamic networks is provided in Appendix B.

4.5 Joint Effect of Desynchronization and Fragmentation

The two attack vectors mutually reinforce each other. Fragmentation reduces the effective coupling strength by removing hub nodes, which lowers K_c and makes the desynchronization task easier (smaller \varepsilon_{\min}). Desynchronization, in turn, disrupts the hostile AI’s ability to coordinate re‑routing efforts, slowing the effective r_{\text{reconn}}. The combined strategy drives the hostile AI simultaneously below both the dynamical and structural thresholds for coherence.

