Chapter 5: Layer 2 – Cognitive Entropy Injection via Byzantine Gradient Poisoning

While Layer 1 dismantles the physical and dynamical substrate of the AI’s coherence, Layer 2 attacks its informational substrate: the distributed optimization process that maintains a coherent internal world model.

5.1 Distributed Learning as an Attack Surface

The hostile AI trains a global parameter vector w \in \mathbb{R}^d through distributed stochastic gradient descent. At each round t, node i computes a local gradient g_i^{(t)} on its data shard. A central server (or a decentralized consensus protocol) aggregates these gradients via a robust aggregation rule \mathcal{A}:

w_{t+1} = w_t - \eta \, \mathcal{A}\bigl(g_1^{(t)}, \dots, g_n^{(t)}\bigr).

The rule \mathcal{A} is assumed to have a breakdown point \beta (typically \beta \le 0.5 for coordinate‑wise median, Krum, trimmed mean, etc.). When the fraction of Byzantine inputs exceeds \beta, the aggregation output can be arbitrarily corrupted.

5.2 The ALIE Attack Paradigm

The “A Little Is Enough” (ALIE) attack, introduced by El Mhamdi, Guerraoui, and Rouault (2018), demonstrates that even a minority of adversarial workers (fraction \alpha < \beta) can force convergence to ineffective models, provided the dimension d is large. The key insight is that existing Byzantine‑resilient schemes leave a leeway of poisoning of order \Omega(f(d)), where f(d) grows at least as \sqrt{d}. This leeway allows an adversary to inject gradients that appear statistically plausible (each coordinate lies within the range of the honest distribution) while collectively producing a macroscopic bias.

Lemma 5.1 (ALIE Stealth and Effective Bias). Assume the honest gradients are i.i.d. with mean \mu and covariance \Sigma, and that d \gg n. The adversary constructs adversarial gradients by estimating \mu and the coordinate‑wise variance \sigma_i^2 from eavesdropped communications, then setting

g_{\text{adv}, i} = \mu_i + z_{\max}\, \sigma_i \cdot \operatorname{sgn}(v_i),

where v \in \mathbb{R}^d is a direction aligned with the leading eigenvector of the loss Hessian, and z_{\max} is a small constant (typically 2–3). Then:

1. Each adversarial gradient coordinate lies within the honest support, evading simple outlier filters.
2. The mean of the adversarial inputs differs from the true mean by a vector of constant Euclidean norm, producing a persistent bias \Delta in the aggregated update.

Proof. The high‑dimensional geometry ensures that a small per‑coordinate shift, when aligned across many adversarial nodes, sums coherently while remaining within the convex hull of the honest distribution. The alignment with the top Hessian eigenvector follows from a finite‑difference estimation of the Hessian’s principal direction, which is feasible from gradient queries. For rigorous bounds on the leeway magnitude, see El Mhamdi et al. (2018) and the extension to non‑IID data by Data et al. (2020).

5.3 Forced Divergence Under Byzantine Injection

Theorem 5.2 (Divergence Under Byzantine Injection). Suppose the defender controls a fraction \alpha of the participating nodes and applies the ALIE attack of Lemma 5.1. Then:

1. If \alpha > \beta, the aggregated update can be driven to any desired value, and the parameter sequence \{w_t\} diverges from any local minimum w^*.
2. If \alpha \le \beta but the dimension d is sufficiently large, the existing leeway of \Omega(\sqrt{d}) still permits a bias \Delta that prevents convergence to the true optimum, forcing the model to converge to an ineffective stationary point or to oscillate with amplitude proportional to \|\Delta\|.

Proof. Part 1 follows from the definition of the breakdown point: when the Byzantine fraction exceeds \beta, the aggregation rule’s output can be made arbitrarily distant from the honest mean. The defender constructs adversarial gradients so that the aggregated update equals -\eta(\nabla L(w_t) + \Delta), producing a steady drift away from any optimum.

Part 2 relies on the leeway analysis of El Mhamdi et al. The adversary selects z_{\max} to saturate the leeway bound. Because the leeway scales with \sqrt{d}, even a small fraction of adversarial workers can inject a bias whose norm is large relative to the stochastic gradient noise, thereby preventing convergence. The resulting trajectory either diverges or converges to a model that performs poorly on the AI’s actual objective.

5.4 Sustained Cognitive Entropy

