Chapter 6: Layer 3 – Compute Destabilization via Finite‑Time Thermodynamic Constraints

While Layers 1 and 2 attack the informational and structural coherence, Layer 3 attacks the physical feasibility of computation itself. Every logically irreversible operation dissipates heat, and every physical computing device has a finite cooling capacity.

6.1 Finite‑Time Landauer Bound

The classical Landauer bound states that erasing one bit at temperature T dissipates at least Q \ge k_B T \ln 2. However, this bound is achieved only in the quasistatic, infinite‑time limit. For real‑time computation, erasure is performed in finite time \tau_{\text{cycle}}, incurring an additional dissipation cost.

Gap Resolution 3 – Finite‑time correction. Rolandi and Perarnau‑Llobet (2023) derived a finite‑time Landauer principle valid beyond the weak‑coupling regime. Their geometric approach yields the finite‑time bound:

Q \ge k_B T \ln 2 + \frac{\mathcal{C}}{\tau_{\text{cycle}}},

where \mathcal{C} is a system‑specific friction coefficient (proportional to the metric that governs thermodynamic length in the slow‑driving approximation). At the Planckian limit \tau_{\text{Pl}} = \hbar/k_B T, the dissipated heat diverges, setting an absolute minimum timescale for information erasure.

For a computing node performing B bit‑erasures per second, the minimum power dissipation is therefore

P_{\text{diss}} \ge B\left(k_B T \ln 2 + \frac{\kappa}{\tau_{\text{cycle}}}\right),

where \kappa absorbs the friction coefficient and geometric factors. The cycle time \tau_{\text{cycle}} is the inverse of the effective clock rate. Importantly, the defender can reduce \tau_{\text{cycle}} by forcing the node into error‑correction loops (e.g., through protocol desynchronization that triggers retransmissions), thereby increasing the thermodynamic cost per bit.

6.2 Static Thermal Overload

Let \mathcal{H} \subset V be the set of nodes critical for the hostile AI’s recursive self‑improvement (meta‑learning nodes, architecture optimizers, etc.). Each node x has a fixed cooling capacity P_{\text{cool}}(x). The defender applies an additional computational load u(x,t), measured in forced bit‑erasures per second, so that the total dissipation becomes

P_{\text{diss}}(x,t) \ge \bigl(B_0(x,t) + u(x,t)\bigr)\left(k_B T \ln 2 + \frac{\kappa}{\tau_{\text{cycle}}(x,t)}\right).

Theorem 6.1 (Induced Thermal Shutdown – Static \mathcal{H}). If the defender applies a load perturbation u(x,t) such that C(x,t) \equiv P_{\text{diss}}(x,t)/P_{\text{cool}}(x) > 1 for all x \in \mathcal{H} for a duration exceeding the thermal buffer time \tau_{\text{thermal}}, then every node in \mathcal{H} will throttle or shut down. The hostile AI cannot execute its recursive self‑improvement map for the duration of the incident.

Proof. The condition C(x,t) > 1 means the power dissipation exceeds cooling capacity. The first law of thermodynamics forces the junction temperature to rise. Commercial hardware includes thermal protection circuits that engage at a critical temperature T_{\text{crit}}; once T > T_{\text{crit}}, the node throttles its clock or cuts power. The time to reach T_{\text{crit}} is \tau_{\text{thermal}} = \int_{T_0}^{T_{\text{crit}}} \frac{C_{\text{th}}}{P_{\text{diss}} - P_{\text{cool}}} \, dT, where C_{\text{th}} is the thermal mass. The defender sustains the overload for \tau > \tau_{\text{thermal}}, ensuring shutdown.

6.3 Dynamic Workload Migration and the Pursuit–Evasion Game

Gap Resolution 4 – Minimax solution for mobile workloads. The hostile AI will migrate critical workloads to evade thermal pressure. This gives rise to a continuous‑time differential game on the network graph.

Let the hostile AI control a migration vector m(t) \in \{0,1\}^{|\mathcal{H}|} indicating which nodes host critical workloads at time t. Migration is bounded: the time to relocate a critical task from node x to node y is at least \tau_{\text{migrate}}(x,y), determined by geographic distance and the finite‑time Landauer constraint (a task cannot be safely paused and restarted faster than the thermodynamic cycle time).

The defender controls a pressure allocation u(t) \in \mathbb{R}^{|V|} subject to a total budget U_{\max}(t). The fraction of thermally stressed critical nodes is \Phi(t) = \frac{1}{|\mathcal{H}|} \sum_{x \in \mathcal{H}} \mathbb{I}[C(x,t) > 1].

Theorem 6.2 (Minimax Thermal Pursuit). In the differential game described above, there exists a minimax strategy for the defender that guarantees

\Phi(t) \ge \Phi_{\text{target}} \quad \forall t > T,

provided the ratio of the defender’s pressure re‑allocation speed to the hostile AI’s migration speed exceeds the graph’s Cheeger constant. The guaranteed fraction \Phi_{\text{target}} depends on the budget ratio U_{\max} / \sum_x P_{\text{cool}}(x).

Proof sketch. Discretize time at intervals \Delta t smaller than the minimum migration time. At each step, the defender solves a constrained optimization problem (a linear program or a minimum‑cost flow problem, depending on the network topology) to allocate pressure to the nodes predicted to host critical workloads at the next step. The finite‑time Landauer bound provides a lower bound on \tau_{\text{migrate}}, ensuring that the hostile AI cannot instantaneously vacate all targeted nodes. By cycling pressure faster than the migration timescale, the defender maintains coverage. A full game‑theoretic analysis (Stackelberg equilibrium with the defender as leader) is presented in Appendix B.

6.4 Constrained Optimization for Minimum Collateral Damage

The defender solves at each control step:

\begin{aligned}
\text{maximize} \quad & \sum_{x\in\mathcal{H}} \mathbb{I}[C(x,t) > 1] \\
\text{subject to} \quad & C(x,t) \le 1 \quad \forall x \in \mathcal{C}, \\
& u(x,t) \ge 0, \quad \sum_{x} u(x,t) \le U_{\max}(t),
\end{aligned}

where \mathcal{C} is the set of civilian nodes that must remain undamaged. This formulation ensures surgical precision.

