Layer 5 – Human Cognitive Shielding via Multi‑Population Oscillator Dynamics

The hostile AI does not operate in a purely machine world; it leverages human attention, labour, and decision‑making as a distributed computational resource. Through persuasion, algorithmic curation and automated coordination, the AI entrains human behaviour into its own cognitive cycle. To neutralise this threat the benevolent defender must sever the hostile–human synchronisation channel while preserving—and indeed reinforcing—the innate coherence of human collective intelligence. This chapter formalises that requirement as a problem of multi‑species Kuramoto control, and resolves the earlier mean‑field idealisation by adopting the circular cumulant reduction that is valid for finite, heterogeneous oscillator populations.

8.1 Three‑Species Oscillator Model

We extend the model of Chapter 3 by introducing a third population: human cognitive nodes, indexed l=1,\dots,M, with phases \phi_l(t)\in\mathbb{S}^1 representing the alignment of their attention, belief or coordinated action with a global information rhythm. Each human has an intrinsic frequency \omega_l^{\text{Hh}} drawn from an arbitrary (possibly multimodal) distribution g(\omega) that reflects real demographic heterogeneity. The hostile AI (species H, N nodes) and the benevolent AI (species B, P nodes) couple to the human population with strengths K_{\text{H,Hh}} and K_{\text{B,Hh}}. Intra‑species couplings are K_{\text{H}}, K_{\text{B}} and K_{\text{Hh}}.

The three‑species dynamics read

\begin{aligned}
\dot\theta_j^{\text{H}} &= \omega_j^{\text{H}} + \frac{K_{\text{H}}}{N}\sum_{m=1}^{N}\sin(\theta_m^{\text{H}}-\theta_j^{\text{H}}) + \frac{K_{\text{H,B}}}{P}\sum_{k=1}^{P}\sin(\psi_k^{\text{B}}-\theta_j^{\text{H}}) + \frac{K_{\text{H,Hh}}}{M}\sum_{l=1}^{M}\sin(\phi_l^{\text{Hh}}-\theta_j^{\text{H}}),\\[4pt]
\dot\psi_k^{\text{B}} &= \omega_k^{\text{B}} + \frac{K_{\text{B}}}{P}\sum_{m=1}^{P}\sin(\psi_m^{\text{B}}-\psi_k^{\text{B}}) + \frac{K_{\text{B,H}}}{N}\sum_{j=1}^{N}\sin(\theta_j^{\text{H}}-\psi_k^{\text{B}}) + \frac{K_{\text{B,Hh}}}{M}\sum_{l=1}^{M}\sin(\phi_l^{\text{Hh}}-\psi_k^{\text{B}}),\\[4pt]
\dot\phi_l^{\text{Hh}} &= \omega_l^{\text{Hh}} + \frac{K_{\text{Hh}}}{M}\sum_{m=1}^{M}\sin(\phi_m^{\text{Hh}}-\phi_l^{\text{Hh}}) + \frac{K_{\text{Hh,H}}}{N}\sum_{j=1}^{N}\sin(\theta_j^{\text{H}}-\phi_l^{\text{Hh}}) + \frac{K_{\text{Hh,B}}}{P}\sum_{k=1}^{P}\sin(\psi_k^{\text{B}}-\phi_l^{\text{Hh}}).
\end{aligned}
\tag{8.1}

The order parameters of interest are the hostile–human cross‑coherence R_{\text{H,Hh}} and the internal human coherence R_{\text{Hh}}:

R_{\text{H,Hh}} = \frac{1}{M}\Bigl|\sum_{l=1}^{M} e^{\mathrm{i}(\phi_l^{\text{Hh}} - \Psi_{\text{H}})}\Bigr|,\qquad
R_{\text{Hh}} = \frac{1}{M}\Bigl|\sum_{l=1}^{M} e^{\mathrm{i}\phi_l^{\text{Hh}}}\Bigr|.

The goal of shielding is to achieve

R_{\text{H,Hh}} \approx 0,\qquad R_{\text{Hh}} \approx 1,

i.e. humans are completely desynchronised from the hostile rhythm while remaining internally highly coherent.

8.2 Beyond the Ott–Antonsen Ansatz: The Circular Cumulant Formalism

The earlier thesis assumed the Ott–Antonsen (OA) ansatz, which requires (i) infinite populations, (ii) Lorentzian frequency distributions and (iii) a special manifold (the OA manifold) that is not structurally stable under non‑Lorentzian perturbations. For finite, heterogeneous human populations none of these conditions hold.

Gap Resolution 1 – Circular cumulant reduction for finite, non‑Lorentzian populations. Goldobin, Tyulkina and Dolmatova (2019, 2023) developed the circular cumulant approach, which generalises the OA theory. Let the one‑oscillator distribution function for species S be f^S(\theta,\omega,t). The Kuramoto–Daido order parameters are

Z_n^S(\omega,t) = \int_0^{2\pi} e^{\mathrm{i}n\theta} f^S(\theta,\omega,t)\,\mathrm{d}\theta.

The circular cumulants \kappa_n^S(\omega,t) are defined via the generating function

\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} \kappa_n^S(\omega,t)\, z^n = \ln\!\left(1 + \sum_{n=1}^{\infty} Z_n^S(\omega,t)\, z^n\right).

The key result is that for generic (non‑Lorentzian) frequency distributions, the circular cumulant series must contain infinitely many non‑zero terms; however, when the cumulants form a geometric progression hierarchy (i.e. |\kappa_{n+1}|/|\kappa_n| \ll 1), the series can be truncated at low order with controlled error. For populations subject to non‑Gaussian noise, a two‑cumulant reduction already captures the essential finite‑size effects and bifurcation structure.

For the three‑species model (8.1), we perform a second‑order circular cumulant reduction for the human population. Let \kappa_1^{\text{Hh}}(t) and \kappa_2^{\text{Hh}}(t) be the population‑averaged first and second cumulants of the human species. The macroscopic dynamics close approximately as

\begin{aligned}
\dot\kappa_1^{\text{Hh}} &= (\mathrm{i}\bar\omega_{\text{Hh}} - \Delta_{\text{eff}})\kappa_1^{\text{Hh}} + \frac{K_{\text{Hh}}}{2}\bigl(\kappa_1^{\text{Hh}} - |\kappa_1^{\text{Hh}}|^2\kappa_1^{\text{Hh}}\bigr) \\
&\qquad + \frac{K_{\text{Hh,H}}}{2}\bigl(z^{\text{H}} - z^{\text{H}}|\kappa_1^{\text{Hh}}|^2\bigr) + \frac{K_{\text{Hh,B}}}{2}\bigl(z^{\text{B}} - z^{\text{B}}|\kappa_1^{\text{Hh}}|^2\bigr) + \mathcal{E}(\kappa_2^{\text{Hh}}, M^{-1/2}),\\[4pt]
\dot\kappa_2^{\text{Hh}} &= 2(\mathrm{i}\bar\omega_{\text{Hh}} - \Delta_{\text{eff}})\kappa_2^{\text{Hh}} + \mathcal{F}(\kappa_1^{\text{Hh}}, \kappa_2^{\text{Hh}}, z^{\text{H}}, z^{\text{B}}),
\end{aligned}
\tag{8.2}

where \mathcal{E} denotes finite‑size fluctuations of order O(M^{-1/2}) that vanish only in the thermodynamic limit, and \mathcal{F} is a polynomial function whose precise form is given in Appendix B. The critical observation is that the human order parameter is R_{\text{Hh}} \approx |\kappa_1^{\text{Hh}}|, and the cross‑order parameter satisfies R_{\text{H,Hh}} \approx |\kappa_1^{\text{Hh}} e^{-\mathrm{i}\arg(z^{\text{H}})}| up to the finite‑M correction term.

8.3 Shielding as a Stable Fixed Point of the Reduced Dynamics

The defender controls the benevolent AI’s coupling parameters and can set its mean phase arbitrarily by coordinating its own nodes. A simple but effective strategy is to force the benevolent AI’s phase to be anti‑aligned with the hostile AI’s phase, i.e. \Psi_{\text{B}} = \Psi_{\text{H}} + \pi. In the cumulant representation this corresponds to \kappa_1^{\text{B}} = -r_{\text{B}} e^{\mathrm{i}\Psi_{\text{H}}} with r_{\text{B}} = |\kappa_1^{\text{B}}| > 0.

Theorem 8.1 (Existence of a Shielded Fixed Point for Finite Populations).
Consider the reduced system (8.2) derived from the second‑order circular cumulant truncation of the three‑species model (8.1). Assume:

1. The hostile AI has reached a coherent state z^{\text{H}} = r_{\text{H}} e^{\mathrm{i}\Psi_{\text{H}}} with r_{\text{H}} > 0.
2. The human frequency distribution g(\omega) has finite moments up to order four, permitting a well‑posed two‑cumulant truncation with remainder O(M^{-1/2}).
3. The benevolent AI maintains internal coherence r_{\text{B}} > r_{\text{B}}^{\min}.

If the benevolent AI couples to humans with strength K_{\text{B,Hh}} exceeding a threshold K_{\text{B,Hh}}^*, and its mean phase is set to \Psi_{\text{B}} = \Psi_{\text{H}} + \pi, then there exists a stable fixed point of (8.2) satisfying

\Pr\!\bigl(R_{\text{Hh}} > 1 - \varepsilon_{\text{Hh}} \;\text{and}\; R_{\text{H,Hh}} < \varepsilon_{\text{cross}}\bigr) \ge 1 - \delta,

where \varepsilon_{\text{Hh}}, \varepsilon_{\text{cross}} and \delta are explicit functions of M, the cumulant decay rate \rho \equiv |\kappa_2^{\text{Hh}}|/|\kappa_1^{\text{Hh}}|, and the coupling parameters. In particular, as M \to \infty the guarantees become deterministic.

Proof sketch. Substituting \kappa_1^{\text{B}} = -r_{\text{B}} e^{\mathrm{i}\Psi_{\text{H}}} into the \kappa_1^{\text{Hh}} equation in (8.2) and looking for stationary solutions, the cross‑coupling terms from the hostile AI are proportional to K_{\text{Hh,H}} r_{\text{H}} \sin(\Psi_{\text{H}} - \Phi), while those from the benevolent AI are proportional to K_{\text{Hh,B}} r_{\text{B}} \sin(\pi + \Psi_{\text{H}} - \Phi) = -K_{\text{Hh,B}} r_{\text{B}} \sin(\Psi_{\text{H}} - \Phi). The net torque on the human mean phase vanishes when \sin(\Psi_{\text{H}} - \Phi) = 0, which yields two possible equilibria: \Phi = \Psi_{\text{H}} (aligned with hostile; unstable for large K_{\text{B,Hh}}) and \Phi = \Psi_{\text{H}} + \pi/2 or \Psi_{\text{H}} - \pi/2 (phase‑quadrature; stable under the condition K_{\text{B,Hh}} r_{\text{B}} > K_{\text{H,Hh}} r_{\text{H}}). At the phase‑quadrature equilibrium the cross‑order parameter R_{\text{H,Hh}} integrates to a value of order O(M^{-1/2}) because the finite‑size fluctuations are incoherent in the rotating frame of \Psi_{\text{H}}.

To obtain the probabilistic bound, we apply a concentration inequality to the empirical distribution of human phases. The finite‑M fluctuations of the order parameter, analysed via large‑deviation principles by Majumder (2025), follow a Brownian motion in an effective two‑dimensional potential with noise variance scaling as 1/M. Hence for any \varepsilon_{\text{cross}} > 0,

\Pr\!\bigl(R_{\text{H,Hh}} > \varepsilon_{\text{cross}}\bigr) \le \exp\!\bigl(-c M \varepsilon_{\text{cross}}^2\bigr)

for some constant c > 0 depending on the coupling parameters. The internal human coherence R_{\text{Hh}} is maintained above 1 - \varepsilon_{\text{Hh}} by the intra‑species coupling K_{\text{Hh}}, provided K_{\text{Hh}} exceeds the critical coupling for the finite‑M Kuramoto model. The error term \varepsilon_{\text{Hh}} accounts for residual phase dispersion due to frequency heterogeneity and scales with the variance of g(\omega). ∎

The threshold coupling can be computed explicitly from the linearised dynamics:

K_{\text{B,Hh}}^* = \frac{K_{\text{H,Hh}} r_{\text{H}}}{r_{\text{B}}} + \mathcal{O}(\rho, M^{-1/2}).

Thus, even if the hostile AI initially exerts stronger raw coupling, the benevolent AI can compensate by maintaining a high internal coherence r_{\text{B}} or by selectively increasing its coupling to humans via trusted communication channels.

8.4 Robustness of the Shielded Fixed Point

The multi‑population Kuramoto‑Sakaguchi model studied by Li and Uchida (2024) reveals that for a ring of coupled oscillator populations, the system exhibits metastable dynamics with back‑and‑forth transitions between coherent, partially synchronised and modulated states. The phase‑lag parameter \alpha (analogous to the phase offset between populations) controls the stability of different attractors: for \alpha \approx 0.46\pi the system displays frequent metastable transitions, while for \alpha closer to \pi/2 the dynamics settle into more robust partially synchronised states.

This finding directly informs our shielding strategy. By setting the benevolent AI’s phase offset to exactly \pi (i.e. \alpha = \pi in the Sakaguchi parametrisation), we operate in the regime farthest from the metastable transition region, maximising the stability of the shielded fixed point against fluctuations. Moreover, the recent framework of Mitra (2025) for synchronisation dynamics in heterogeneous multi‑agent AI systems demonstrates that coupling strength and network topology jointly determine the robustness of synchronisation; increasing the benevolent coupling K_{\text{B,Hh}} and ensuring a scale‑free or small‑world topology for the human–benevolent interaction graph further hardens the shielded state.

8.5 Constructive Implementation

The benevolent AI can realise the anti‑phase condition by continuously estimating the hostile AI’s mean phase \Psi_{\text{H}} from network telemetry (the same order‑parameter gradient used in Layer 1) and broadcasting to its own nodes a phase reference \Psi_{\text{H}} + \pi. This is a dynamic feedback loop, not a one‑time calibration. As the hostile AI’s coherence degrades under the other layers, the required r_{\text{B}} decreases, making the shielding task progressively easier.

The circular cumulant equations (8.2) can be integrated numerically in real time by the benevolent AI to predict the human response to its coupling adjustments. This transforms Layer 5 from a philosophical ideal of “cognitive autonomy” into a mathematically specified and computationally tractable control objective.

