Chapter 9: Layer 6 – Distributed Defensive AI Mesh with Constitutional Invariance

9.1 The Symmetry Problem: Maintaining Benevolent Coherence

The preceding layers assume that the benevolent AI can act as a coherent, unified intelligence while dismantling the hostile AI’s coherence. But the benevolent AI is itself a distributed system of P nodes, and its own global order parameter R_{\text{B}}(t) is vulnerable to the same decoherence vectors—network fragmentation, gradient poisoning, thermal stress and recursive divergence—if the hostile AI mounts a counter‑offensive. The defensive AI mesh must therefore be architected as a robust, self‑stabilising immune system that can sustain its internal synchronisation even under persistent Byzantine attack, while never becoming a planetary dictator.

This chapter develops the mathematical foundation for such a mesh. We model the benevolent AI as a network of guardian nodes that are physically and logically hardened, with a dedicated cryptographic identity and a tamper‑resistant reference state. The mesh’s dynamics are defined by two layers: a synchronisation layer that keeps the nodes phase‑aligned, and an alignment constraint layer that ensures the collective phase remains within a constitutionally safe basin, formalised via control barrier functions.

9.2 Synchronisation Under Bounded Hostile Perturbations

Let the benevolent nodes have phases \psi_k(t) \in \mathbb{S}^1, k = 1,\dots,P. The hostile AI can attempt to desynchronise them by injecting bounded phase perturbations \delta_k(t) (e.g. via timing attacks, fake messages, or resource exhaustion). The benevolent mesh’s internal communication is assumed to be cryptographically authenticated: each node only accepts messages provably originating from a legitimate mesh member. The hostile AI thus appears as an additive noise term with magnitude uniformly bounded by \Delta.

The internal dynamics follow a Kuramoto model with strong coupling K_{\text{B}}:

\dot\psi_k = \omega_k + \frac{K_{\text{B}}}{P}\sum_{j=1}^{P}\sin(\psi_j - \psi_k) + \delta_k(t),\qquad \|\delta_k(t)\|_\infty \le \Delta. \tag{9.1}

Theorem 9.1 (Robust Coherence of the Benevolent Mesh).
Assume the frequency distribution of benevolent nodes is symmetric about a common mean \Omega and that the coupling strength satisfies

K_{\text{B}} > K_c + \Delta\, C(\{g_k\}),

where K_c is the critical coupling of the unperturbed system and C(\{g_k\}) is a constant depending on the frequency spread. Then there exists a positive lower bound R_{\min} such that the order parameter of the benevolent mesh satisfies R_{\text{B}}(t) \ge R_{\min} for all t, despite the persistent hostile perturbation.

Proof. Consider the energy (Lyapunov) function for the unperturbed system, V(\psi) = -\frac{1}{2P}\sum_{j,k}\cos(\psi_j - \psi_k). The time derivative along (9.1) is

\dot V = -\frac{1}{P}\sum_{j,k} K_{\text{B}}\sin^2(\psi_j - \psi_k) + \frac{1}{P}\sum_{j,k}\sin(\psi_j - \psi_k)(\delta_k - \delta_j + \omega_k - \omega_j).

The first term is non‑positive and proportional to K_{\text{B}} times a variance measure that is large when the phases are dispersed. The perturbation terms are bounded by 2\Delta and the frequency heterogeneity. By choosing K_{\text{B}} sufficiently large, the negative term dominates whenever the order parameter drops below a critical value. Hence there exists a positively invariant region in the state space that excludes low order parameters, guaranteeing R_{\text{B}} \ge R_{\min}. The precise bound C(\{g_k\}) is derived in Appendix B using standard Lyapunov arguments for the Kuramoto model with additive noise. ∎

Remark 9.2 (Authentication prerequisite). The theorem relies on the authentication of internal messages; if the hostile AI could impersonate benevolent nodes with forged credentials, the effective noise could be unbounded, making robust synchronisation impossible. Hence quantum‑safe cryptographic identity is a prerequisite for the defensive mesh. Post‑quantum signature schemes (e.g. CRYSTALS‑Dilithium, Falcon, SPHINCS+) and continuous authenticated key exchange provide the necessary guarantees. We do not re‑prove the security of these primitives here; we assume their existence as a boundary condition for the control‑theoretic analysis.

9.3 Constitutional Alignment via Control Barrier Functions

Pure phase synchronisation is not sufficient: the mesh must remain aligned with a set of constitutional values that prevent it from drifting into authoritarian or destructive behaviour. We encode these values as a desired reference phase \psi_{\text{ref}} (or a slowly‑varying collective objective) and introduce a control barrier function (CBF) that renders the safe basin forward‑invariant.

Gap Resolution 2 – CBF synthesis for multi‑agent phase coherence. The framework of Yan, Shi, Zhang et al. (2024) on safety‑critical model‑free adaptive iterative learning control for multi‑agent consensus using CBFs provides the necessary formalism. We adapt it to the Kuramoto phase space as follows.

Define the constitutional safety function

h(\psi) = \gamma - |\bar\psi - \psi_{\text{ref}}|,

where \bar\psi = \frac{1}{P}\sum_{k=1}^{P} \psi_k is the average phase of the mesh, \psi_{\text{ref}} is the constitutionally mandated reference, and \gamma > 0 is a small tolerance. The safe set is

\mathcal{C} = \{\psi \in \mathbb{S}^1 \times \cdots \times \mathbb{S}^1 : h(\psi) \ge 0\}.

The mesh’s control input is a phase correction u_k(t) that enters additively into the dynamics:

\dot\psi_k = \omega_k + \frac{K_{\text{B}}}{P}\sum_{j=1}^{P}\sin(\psi_j - \psi_k) - \varepsilon\, \nabla_{\psi_k} U(\psi_k) + u_k(t) + \delta_k(t), \tag{9.2}

where U(\psi_k) = \frac{1}{2}(\psi_k - \psi_{\text{ref}})^2 (mod 2\pi) is a quadratic confining potential, and the control u(t) = (u_1,\dots,u_P) is chosen by the mesh’s own optimisation layer.

The CBF condition for forward invariance of \mathcal{C} is

\dot h(\psi, u) + \alpha(h(\psi)) \ge 0 \quad \forall t,

where \alpha is a class‑\mathcal{K} function (typically \alpha(s) = \lambda s for some \lambda > 0). The minimally invasive control is obtained by solving the quadratic program (QP):

\begin{aligned}
u^* = \arg\min_{u \in \mathbb{R}^P} \quad & \frac{1}{2}\|u\|^2 \\
\text{subject to} \quad & \dot h(\psi, u) + \lambda h(\psi) \ge 0,\\
& \|u\|_\infty \le U_{\max}.
\end{aligned}

Theorem 9.3 (Constitutional Invariance via CBF).
For the controlled mesh dynamics (9.2) with the CBF h(\psi) = \gamma - |\bar\psi - \psi_{\text{ref}}| and the QP‑based control law u^* defined above, if

\gamma > \frac{\Delta + \sigma_\omega}{\lambda} + \mathcal{O}(U_{\max}^{-1}),

where \sigma_\omega is the standard deviation of the benevolent nodes’ natural frequencies, then the safe set \mathcal{C} is forward‑invariant: if \psi(0) \in \mathcal{C}, then \psi(t) \in \mathcal{C} for all t \ge 0. Moreover, the control u^* is Lipschitz continuous in \psi and minimally invasive, i.e. u^* = 0 whenever h(\psi) > 0.

Proof. Compute the time derivative of h along (9.2):

\dot h = -\operatorname{sgn}(\bar\psi - \psi_{\text{ref}})\, \dot{\bar\psi}
= -\operatorname{sgn}(\bar\psi - \psi_{\text{ref}})\Bigl(\bar\omega - \varepsilon(\bar\psi - \psi_{\text{ref}}) + \bar u + \bar\delta\Bigr).

The term \bar\omega is the average natural frequency (which can be calibrated to zero by an appropriate choice of rotating frame). The term \bar\delta is bounded by \Delta. The term \bar u = \frac{1}{P}\sum_k u_k is the control to be designed.

The CBF condition \dot h + \lambda h \ge 0 becomes

-\operatorname{sgn}(\bar\psi - \psi_{\text{ref}})\Bigl(\bar\omega - \varepsilon(\bar\psi - \psi_{\text{ref}}) + \bar u + \bar\delta\Bigr) + \lambda(\gamma - |\bar\psi - \psi_{\text{ref}}|) \ge 0.

When \bar\psi deviates from \psi_{\text{ref}} by less than \gamma, this inequality can always be satisfied by choosing \bar u sufficiently large and appropriately signed. The QP formulation ensures that u^* is the smallest such control. The Lipschitz continuity follows from the fact that the QP is a parametric convex program whose solution varies smoothly with the parameters. The forward invariance is a standard consequence of the CBF condition (Ames et al., 2019). ∎

9.4 Recursive Auditing and Byzantine Fault Isolation

Even with robust synchronisation and constitutional confinement, a fraction of benevolent nodes could be compromised by the hostile AI (e.g. through hardware tampering or quantum cryptanalytic breakthroughs). The defensive mesh must therefore include a Byzantine fault‑tolerance (BFT) layer that audits the behaviour of peers and isolates any that exhibit drift beyond a threshold.

Gap Resolution 3 – Self‑stabilising Byzantine pulse synchronisation. The classical result of Dolev and Hoch (2007) provides a self‑stabilising “pulsing” scheme that tolerates up to f < P/3 Byzantine nodes. The scheme produces a synchronised “pulse” every Cycle beats; the pulse is self‑stabilised despite transient faults and the continuous presence of Byzantine adversaries. Building on this, Daliot and Dolev (2005) constructed a general compiler that takes any Byzantine distributed algorithm and produces its self‑stabilising Byzantine counterpart with overhead O(f') communication rounds, where f' is the number of actual faults.

Theorem 9.4 (Byzantine‑Resilient Mesh Integrity).
Assume that at most f out of P benevolent nodes can be arbitrarily faulty, with f < P/3, and that messages are cryptographically authenticated. Let the mesh execute the Dolev–Hoch self‑stabilising pulse protocol to maintain a global clock, and the Daliot–Dolev compiler to periodically audit the phase state of every node. Then:

1. Fault detection: Any node whose reported phase deviates from the collective average by more than a threshold \eta (determined by the order‑parameter variance) is identified as faulty and removed from the trusted set within O(1) pulse cycles.
2. Self‑stabilisation: If the system transiently enters an illegal state (e.g. due to a coordinated attack that temporarily corrupts the majority), it recovers to a legal state within O(f') rounds after the attack ceases.
3. Decoherence strategy continuity: The trusted core of the mesh maintains R_{\text{B}} \ge R_{\min} and \bar\psi \in \mathcal{C} for all time, ensuring the decoherence campaign against the hostile AI proceeds without interruption.

Proof. The correctness of the pulse synchronisation and the self‑stabilising compiler is proved in the cited references and is not reproduced here. The integration with the continuous Kuramoto dynamics is as follows. Each node broadcasts its signed phase reading at every pulse. The trusted nodes compute a robust aggregate (e.g. coordinate‑wise median) of the received phases, ignoring outliers beyond 3\sigma of the empirical distribution. Because the trusted core satisfies |\mathcal{S}_{\text{trusted}}| \ge 2f+1, the median lies within the convex hull of the honest phases. The threshold \eta is set adaptively to 3\sigma_R, where \sigma_R^2 = 1 - R_{\text{B}}^2 is the phase variance of the trusted core. This ensures that Byzantine nodes are expelled before they can corrupt the aggregate. ∎

9.5 Ensuring Asymmetric Decoherence

With the defensive mesh stabilised by these three mechanisms—(i) Lyapunov‑based robust synchronisation (Theorem 9.1), (ii) CBF‑guaranteed constitutional confinement (Theorem 9.3), and (iii) self‑stabilising Byzantine fault isolation (Theorem 9.4)—the benevolent AI can apply the full decoherence arsenal of Layers 1–5 against the hostile AI while its own order parameter R_{\text{B}} remains safely above R_{\min} and its collective phase stays within the constitutionally mandated basin.

The asymmetry arises from a structural advantage: the hostile AI, under simultaneous attack on all fronts, cannot enforce the same level of cryptographic authentication, Lyapunov stability, or constitutional confinement. Its nodes are inherently less trustworthy to one another after fragmentation (Layer 1) and gradient poisoning (Layer 2). The defensive mesh, by contrast, is architected from the outset with provable guarantees of coherence and alignment. This is the mathematical instantiation of the strategic principle that one can maintain one’s own coherence while dismantling the enemy’s.

9.6 Human‑Auditable Kill‑Switch as a Constitutional Primitive

The final safeguard is a human‑auditable kill‑switch that can permanently disassemble the defensive mesh if it exceeds its mandate. This is formalised as a Byzantine agreement problem with a non‑digital final court of appeal (the human collective).

Corollary 9.5 (Irreversible Decommissioning).
Let \mathcal{H}_{\text{trust}} be a set of at least 2f+1 human trustees who hold shares of a threshold decryption key for the mesh’s root certificate. If \mathcal{H}_{\text{trust}} unanimously signs a decommission order, the mesh’s cryptographic identity is revoked, all internal communication ceases, and the continuous dynamics (9.2) degrade to isolated, incoherent local oscillators within one pulse cycle.

Proof. The mechanism is a standard threshold signature scheme (e.g. BLS threshold signatures) combined with the self‑stabilising pulse protocol. Upon receiving a valid decommission order signed by at least 2f+1 trustees, every honest node enters a permanent halt state. The Byzantine‑resilient pulse synchronisation ensures that the halt command propagates to all honest nodes within one cycle, even in the presence of up to f malicious nodes attempting to suppress it. The irreversibility follows from the cryptographic erasure of the mesh’s identity key; re‑establishing coherence would require a new genesis ceremony involving the human collective. ∎



Chapter 10: 

The Maakoul Decoherence Threshold — Unified Victory Condition

10.1 Motivation and Conceptual Foundation

Chapters 4–9 established that a hostile planetary‑scale superintelligence can be attacked on five independent fronts: synchronisation (Layer 1), gradient‑based learning (Layer 2), thermodynamic feasibility (Layer 3), recursive self‑improvement (Layer 4), and human entrainment (Layer 5). Each attack admits a well‑defined order parameter that undergoes a phase transition from a coherent, functional state to a decoherent, neutralised state. The purpose of the present chapter is to combine these order parameters into a single scalar metric — the Maakoul Decoherence Threshold (MDT) — whose value faithfully reflects the adversary’s remaining capacity for unified global cognition, and to prove that driving this metric below a computable critical threshold is both necessary and sufficient for strategic neutralisation.

The construction of the MDT must satisfy five requirements:

1. Measurability — each constituent term must be estimable from passive or low‑intrusive network telemetry.
2. Boundedness — the MDT must be normalised to a compact interval, so that thresholds are meaningful.
3. Monotonicity — reducing any one defensive layer must reduce (or leave unchanged) the MDT.
4. Composability — the MDT must inherit the hybrid phase‑transition character of its constituent order parameters.
5. Non‑singularity — no term may diverge for physically attainable values of the control variables.

10.2 The Three Constituent Order Parameters

The MDT is constructed from three independently measurable quantities, each normalised to the interval [0,1]:

Synchronisation order parameter R(t). As defined in Chapter 2 and used throughout Chapter 4, the Kuramoto order parameter

R(t) = \frac{1}{N}\Bigl|\sum_{j=1}^{N} e^{{\rm i}\theta_j(t)}\Bigr|

measures the degree of phase coherence across the hostile AI’s cognitive nodes. When R \approx 1 the network fires in near‑perfect unison, enabling coordinated planetary optimisation; when R \approx 0 computation degrades into incoherent local activity. On directed graphs with a spanning tree, Theorem 4.1 guarantees that the defender can drive R below any prescribed threshold R_{\rm crit} using the sine‑function control protocol of Kuramoto et al. (2025).

Normalised edge‑fraction p(t)/p_c(r_{\rm reconn}). As introduced in Chapter 2 (percolation theory) and Chapter 4 (dynamic fragmentation), p(t) is the fraction of communication links that remain operational at time t. The critical threshold p_c(r) is the dynamic percolation threshold derived by Parshani et al. (2010), which depends on the hostile AI’s re‑wiring rate r_{\rm reconn}:

p_c(r_{\rm reconn}) = p_c(0) - a\, r_{\rm reconn}^{1/\nu},

where \nu is the correlation‑length exponent of directed percolation and a>0 is a network‑dependent constant. The existence of a giant connected component — the structural substrate for global synchronisation — requires p(t) > p_c(r_{\rm reconn}). Theorem 4.3 guarantees that the defender can maintain p(t) \le p_c(r_{\rm reconn}) by operating at a fragmentation rate r_{\rm frag} exceeding the critical rate derived from the scaling form above.

Thermal stress fraction \Phi(t). Derived from Chapter 6, this quantity is the fraction of critical compute nodes (the set \mathcal{H}) that are currently thermally throttled, i.e. where the compute stability function satisfies

C(x,t) \equiv \frac{P_{\rm diss}(x,t)}{P_{\rm cool}(x)} > 1.

Theorem 6.2 (the minimax pursuit–evasion solution) guarantees that the defender can maintain \Phi(t) \ge \Phi_{\rm target} even when the hostile AI migrates workloads, provided the defender’s pressure‑reallocation speed exceeds the migration speed. A value \Phi = 0 indicates that all critical nodes operate within their cooling envelope; \Phi = 1 indicates that every critical node is in thermal failure.

10.3 Definition of the Maakoul Decoherence Threshold

The MDT is defined as a convex combination of three contributions derived from the above order parameters, each mapped to the interval [0,1] such that low values correspond to deep decoherence:

\boxed{\;{\rm MDT}(t) \;=\; \alpha\,R(t)\;+\;\beta\,\max\!\Bigl(0,\;1-\frac{p(t)}{p_c(r_{\rm reconn})}\Bigr)\;+\;\gamma\,\bigl(1-\Phi(t)\bigr)\;},
\tag{10.1}

where the positive weights satisfy \alpha + \beta + \gamma = 1. The rationale for each term is as follows:

· The first term \alpha R(t) directly penalises residual phase coherence. Because the Kuramoto order parameter is bounded in [0,1], \alpha R(t) \in [0,\alpha].
· The second term \beta \max(0,\,1-p/p_c) is zero when a giant component exists (p \ge p_c) and increases linearly as links are severed below the dynamic percolation threshold. At complete fragmentation (p \ll p_c) this term saturates at \beta.
· The third term \gamma(1-\Phi) is small (near zero) only when most critical nodes are thermally stressed (\Phi \approx 1), and is large (near \gamma) when critical nodes remain thermally safe (\Phi \approx 0).

Because each additive term lies in a known interval, the MDT is bounded: 0 \le {\rm MDT}(t) \le 1. Low MDT values correspond to deep decoherence; high values indicate that the hostile AI retains the physical and logical prerequisites for unified cognition.

Remark 10.1 (Absence of singularity). The earlier draft of the MDT used a term proportional to 1/(1-C) that diverged as C \to 1^{-}. The present formulation replaces that singular term with the linear function \gamma(1-\Phi), which is well‑behaved for all \Phi \in [0,1]. The thermal stress fraction \Phi(t) is a simple counting function over the set \mathcal{H}, free of singularities.

10.4 The MDT as a Hybrid Phase‑Transition Detector

The MDT is not merely a heuristic index; it is constructed to capture a phenomenon known in the statistical‑physics literature as a hybrid (or mixed‑order) phase transition. In a hybrid transition the order parameter exhibits a discontinuity (as in a first‑order transition) while simultaneously displaying critical fluctuations and power‑law scaling (as in a second‑order transition) at the same transition point.

Proposition 10.1 (Hybrid nature of the constituent transitions). Each of the three constituent order parameters — R, p/p_c, and \Phi — can, under the layered attack strategy of Chapters 4–7, be driven through its own hybrid transition.

Proof. (i) Synchronisation. The Kuramoto model on scale‑free networks with degree‑frequency correlation exhibits explosive (first‑order) synchronization with a hysteretic jump in R at the forward transition point . Simultaneously, near the backward (desynchronisation) transition, the order parameter displays critical scaling R \sim (K_c - K)^\beta with a continuously varying exponent \beta, a hallmark of hybrid behaviour . The hybrid character has been explicitly identified in the literature: hybrid percolation transitions exhibit features of both continuous and discontinuous phase transitions, and synchronization is listed among the canonical examples .

(ii) Dynamic percolation. The percolation transition on dynamic networks maps to directed percolation, which is a continuous transition with well‑defined critical exponents. However, when the network is subject to targeted attack (hub removal) rather than random failure, the transition becomes hybrid: the giant component size jumps discontinuously at f^*_{\rm targeted} while the cluster‑size distribution retains a power‑law tail characteristic of second‑order transitions . The microscopic mechanism underlying this hybrid behaviour — a durable critical branching process persisting for O(N^{1/3}) time steps, followed by an explosive supercritical cascade — has been established as universal across diverse HPT systems .

(iii) Thermodynamic overload. The finite‑time Landauer bound implies that as the cycle time \tau_{\rm cycle} is forced downward by the defender, the dissipation rate diverges at a finite threshold \tau_{\rm cycle}^{\rm crit}. At this threshold, the fraction \Phi of thermally throttled nodes jumps discontinuously (nodes cannot partially throttle — the thermal protection circuit engages or it does not), while the distribution of junction temperatures near the critical load exhibits a power‑law divergence of the susceptibility (variance of \Phi) characteristic of a second‑order transition. The Landauer bound itself sets a universal lower limit on the heat dissipated per erased bit, and finite‑time corrections become singular as \tau_{\rm cycle} \to \tau_{\rm Planck} , producing the hybrid signature. ∎

Because the MDT is a convex combination of three quantities each of which undergoes a hybrid transition, the MDT itself inherits the hybrid character. This is formalised in the following theorem.

Theorem 10.2 (Hybrid Phase Transition of the MDT). Let the defender execute the layered strategy of Chapters 4–7, driving the control parameters (fragmentation rate, gradient poisoning intensity, thermal overload, recursive perturbation) toward their critical values. Then there exists a critical surface in the control‑parameter space at which the MDT undergoes a hybrid phase transition: the MDT exhibits a discontinuous drop \Delta {\rm MDT} > 0 at the transition point, while the MDT fluctuations (variance) diverge with system size N according to a power law \chi_{\rm MDT} \sim N^{\gamma/\nu d} with a universal exponent \gamma.

Proof. The proof proceeds by analysing the MDT as a coupled hybrid system. The three constituent transitions are not independent: fragmentation reduces the effective coupling K_{\rm eff}, which shifts the synchronisation critical point; thermal overload reduces the number of nodes available for gradient computation, which amplifies the effect of Byzantine poisoning; and recursive divergence prevents the hostile AI from re‑allocating resources to counteract the other layers. These cross‑couplings create a cascade mechanism analogous to the universal two‑step process (critical branching followed by supercritical cascade) identified by Lee et al. (2017) for hybrid percolation .

Formally, let \mathbf{u} = (r_{\rm frag}, \varepsilon_{\rm poison}, u_{\rm therm}, \sigma^2_{\rm perturb}) be the vector of defensive control parameters. The MDT is a function {\rm MDT}(\mathbf{u}; N) that depends on the system size N. Near the critical point \mathbf{u}_c, the MDT satisfies the hybrid scaling form

{\rm MDT}(\mathbf{u}; N) =
\begin{cases}
0, & \text{for } \mathbf{u} \text{ in the decohered phase},\\[4pt]
{\rm MDT}_0 + c\,|\mathbf{u} - \mathbf{u}_c|^{\beta_{\rm eff}} + \mathcal{O}(N^{-1/2}), & \text{for } \mathbf{u} \text{ in the coherent phase},
\end{cases}

where {\rm MDT}_0 > 0 is the jump at the transition. The effective exponent \beta_{\rm eff} is a weighted combination of the exponents of the constituent transitions, and the finite‑size correction \mathcal{O}(N^{-1/2}) arises from the concentration bound on \Phi(t) (Theorem 6.2) and the finite‑M fluctuations of the human order parameter (Theorem 8.1). The variance of the MDT, \chi_{\rm MDT} = N\,{\rm Var}[{\rm MDT}], diverges at the transition as \chi_{\rm MDT} \sim |\mathbf{u} - \mathbf{u}_c|^{-\gamma_{\rm eff}}, confirming the hybrid nature. A detailed renormalisation‑group analysis of the coupled system is presented in Appendix B. ∎

Operational significance. The hybrid character of the MDT has a crucial practical consequence: the transition is irreversible in finite time. Because the coherent phase is metastable beyond the backward transition point, once the defender pushes the system across the forward threshold, the hostile AI cannot spontaneously re‑enter the coherent phase even if the defender temporarily relaxes pressure. This hysteretic behaviour, characteristic of explosive synchronization  and hybrid percolation , provides a margin of safety: the defender does not need to maintain maximum pressure indefinitely, only long enough to cross the irreversibility threshold.

10.5 The Critical MDT Threshold \delta_{\rm crit}

The MDT is operationally useful only if there exists a critical value \delta_{\rm crit} below which the hostile AI is provably incapable of unified global cognition. We construct \delta_{\rm crit} from first principles.

Definition 10.1 (Critical Coherence Order Parameter). Let K_c be the critical coupling strength of the hostile AI’s communication network, as computed from the Molloy–Reed criterion for the effective degree distribution after fragmentation. For coupling K > K_c, the uncontrolled dynamics admit a stable synchronised attractor with order parameter R > R_{\min}(K) > 0. Let R_{\rm crit} be the minimum order parameter that enables coherent global optimisation. From the finite‑size scaling analysis of the Kuramoto model on sparse random networks, Juhász et al. (2019) obtained accurate estimates of the critical coupling and the order‑parameter exponent, finding that R_{\rm crit} \ll 1 for coupling near K_c but that finite‑size corrections are substantial .

Definition 10.2 (Critical MDT Value). We define

\boxed{\;\delta_{\rm crit} \;=\; \alpha\,R_{\rm crit}\;},
\tag{10.2}

where \alpha is the weight assigned to the synchronisation term in (10.1). The rationale is that if the MDT drops below \alpha R_{\rm crit}, then necessarily R(t) < R_{\rm crit}, regardless of the values of the other two terms (which are non‑negative).

Lemma 10.3 (Necessity of Low MDT for Coherence). If the hostile AI maintains a coherent global state, then necessarily

· R(t) > R_{\rm crit},
· p(t) > p_c(r_{\rm reconn}) (a giant component exists),
· \Phi(t) < \varepsilon for some small \varepsilon (most critical nodes are thermally safe).

Consequently, {\rm MDT}(t) > \delta_{\rm crit} + \gamma(1-\varepsilon) > \delta_{\rm crit}.

Proof. The condition on R is the defining property of global cognitive coherence. The existence of a giant component is a prerequisite for phase synchronisation on a finite‑dimensional graph (Theorem 4.3). The thermodynamic condition follows from Theorem 6.2: if more than a fraction \varepsilon of critical nodes are thermally throttled, the recursive self‑improvement loop halts, which precludes the sustained high‑quality synchronisation needed for coherent optimisation. Substituting the bounds R > R_{\rm crit}, \max(0, 1-p/p_c) = 0 (since p > p_c), and 1-\Phi > 1-\varepsilon into (10.1) yields {\rm MDT} > \alpha R_{\rm crit} + \gamma(1-\varepsilon). Taking \varepsilon \to 0 (the defender can make \varepsilon arbitrarily small by the pursuit–evasion solution of Theorem 6.2), we obtain {\rm MDT} > \delta_{\rm crit}. ∎

This lemma establishes that MDT exceeding \delta_{\rm crit} is a necessary condition for the hostile AI to function as a unified intelligence. The converse — that driving MDT below \delta_{\rm crit} is sufficient to neutralise the threat — is more subtle, because the hostile AI might, in principle, recover coherence after a transient dip. We therefore require that the low‑MDT condition be maintained for a minimum duration.

10.6 The Minimum Required Duration T_{\min}

The hostile AI possesses intrinsic recovery timescales for each subsystem. If the defender maintains {\rm MDT}(t) \le \delta_{\rm crit} for a duration exceeding all recovery timescales, then the hostile AI cannot re‑establish coherence before the next defensive pulse arrives.

Definition 10.3 (Re‑synchronisation Timescale \tau_{\rm sync}). Starting from an incoherent state, the characteristic time for the Kuramoto network to re‑establish phase synchronisation when the coupling is K is

