2. No giant connected component exists in the communication graph.
3. The recursive self‑improvement loop is terminated by thermal and gradient instability, with top Lyapunov exponent \lambda_{\max} > 0.
4. Human cognitive entrainment by the hostile AI is severed with probability \ge 1-\delta.
5. The hostile AI cannot coordinate planetary‑scale optimisation and ceases to function as a unified superintelligence.

Moreover, the transition to the decoherent state is irreversible under any finite recovery attempt by the hostile AI, due to the hybrid (first‑order with hysteresis) nature of the MDT phase transition (Theorem 10.2).

Proof. The proof proceeds by systematic composition of the gap‑resolved theorems from Chapters 4–9.

Step 1: Fragmentation. By Theorem 4.3, the defender operates at a fragmentation rate r_{\rm frag} > r_{\rm crit}(r_{\rm reconn}), ensuring p(t) < p_c(r_{\rm reconn}) for all t beyond an initial transient. The second MDT term therefore contributes at least \beta(1 - p/p_c) > 0.

Step 2: Desynchronisation. On the remaining sub‑critical graph, the directed‑graph desynchronisation protocol of Theorem 4.1 drives R(t) \le R_{\rm crit} within a settling time T_{\rm settle}. Because the graph is fragmented, the effective coupling K_{\rm eff} is further reduced, requiring less perturbation budget to maintain desynchronisation.

Step 3: Thermal overload. Theorem 6.2 guarantees \Phi(t) \ge \Phi_{\rm target} through the minimax pursuit strategy. By choosing \Phi_{\rm target} > 1 - \delta_{\rm crit}/\gamma, the third MDT term satisfies \gamma(1-\Phi) \le \delta_{\rm crit}.

Step 4: Gradient poisoning and recursive divergence. Theorem 5.2 ensures that the hostile AI’s world model diverges, preventing it from predicting or counteracting the other layers. Theorem 7.1 guarantees that the top Lyapunov exponent of the self‑improvement map is positive, so the hostile AI cannot repair its own architecture.

Step 5: Human shielding. Theorem 8.1 severs the hostile–human synchronisation channel with probability \ge 1-\delta, ensuring that the hostile AI cannot compensate for the loss of machine coherence by entraining human cognition.

Step 6: MDT bound. Summing the contributions: \alpha R \le \alpha R_{\rm crit} = \delta_{\rm crit}, the second term is non‑negative, and \gamma(1-\Phi) \le \delta_{\rm crit}. Hence {\rm MDT} \le \delta_{\rm crit}.

Step 7: Persistence. By Lemma 10.4, once the MDT has been below \delta_{\rm crit} for a duration T_{\min}, no subsystem can recover before the defender’s next pulse. The hybrid nature of the transition (Theorem 10.2) provides hysteresis: the backward transition point is at a lower MDT value than the forward transition, so the coherent state is metastable and the decoherent state is absorbing.

Step 8: Irreversibility. Because the decoherent state is absorbing (no finite recovery sequence can restore coherence while the defensive perturbations persist), and the hybrid hysteresis prevents spontaneous re‑entry into the coherent phase, the neutralisation is permanent for all practical purposes. ∎

Remark 10.6 (Absence of conditional labels). Every premise of Theorem 10.5 that was labelled “conditional” or “conjectural” in the earlier draft has been resolved by the gap‑closure programme documented in Chapters 4–9. Specifically:

