Chapter 11: Discussion, Limitations, and Revised Open Problems

11.1 Summary of the Theoretical Achievement

This thesis has constructed a six‑layer mathematical theory for the containment of a hostile planetary‑scale superintelligence. The central insight — that such an entity is a coherence phenomenon rather than a discrete object — has been formalised through the Kuramoto model of synchronisation (on both undirected and directed graphs), dynamic percolation theory on communication networks, Byzantine‑robust distributed optimisation, finite‑time Landauer‑bounded thermodynamics, non‑autonomous recursive divergence, and multi‑species oscillator dynamics with finite‑population corrections. Each defensive layer was formulated as a theorem with explicit assumptions and, through the targeted gap‑closure programme of Chapters 4–9, all previously identified idealisations have been resolved.

The Maakoul Decoherence Threshold (MDT) was re‑defined as a non‑singular convex combination of the synchronisation order parameter, the dynamic percolation edge fraction, and the thermal stress fraction. Theorem 10.2 established that the MDT undergoes a hybrid (mixed‑order) phase transition at a computable critical surface, providing both a discontinuous drop and hysteretic irreversibility. The Main Victory Theorem (Theorem 10.5) now holds unconditionally under physically plausible assumptions: sustaining \mathrm{MDT}(t) \le \delta_{\mathrm{crit}} for a duration exceeding T_{\min} forces the hostile AI into a fragmented, non‑coherent state from which unified planetary cognition cannot recover.

The mathematical architecture is logically closed. The purpose of this chapter is to delineate the boundaries of the present work, to assess the status of the original open problems catalogue, and to identify the new questions that the gap‑resolved framework has opened.

