Appendix B: Supplementary Proofs and Derivations

B.1 Directed‑Graph Lyapunov Function for Desynchronisation (Theorem 4.1)

Consider the directed Kuramoto network with control u_j(t):

\dot\theta_j = \omega_j + \sum_{k: (k,j)\in\vec{E}} K_{jk} \sin(\theta_k - \theta_j) + u_j(t).

Assume the graph contains a directed spanning tree rooted at some node r. Let \theta^{\text{ref}} be a target phase that the defender wants the nodes to avoid; the defender’s goal is to drive the system away from the synchronised cluster near \theta^{\text{ref}}. Define the error variable

e_j = 1 - \cos(\theta_j - \theta^{\text{ref}}).

The candidate Lyapunov function is

V_{\text{dir}}(\theta) = \sum_{j=1}^{N} e_j + \frac{1}{2} \sum_{j=1}^{N} \sum_{k: (k,j)\in\vec{E}} \bigl(1 - \cos(\theta_j - \theta_k)\bigr).

The first sum penalises alignment with the reference; the second penalises pairwise coherence. We design the control as

u_j(t) = -c_1 \sum_{k \in \mathcal{N}_j^{\text{in}}} \sin(\theta_j - \theta_k) - c_2 \,\operatorname{sgn}\!\bigl(\sin(\theta_j - \theta^{\text{ref}})\bigr),

with c_1, c_2 > 0. The sign function is understood as a set‑valued map to handle the discontinuity; we can alternatively use a smooth approximation \tanh(\cdot / \epsilon) for practical implementation; the Lyapunov argument is unaffected because the discontinuity occurs only on a set of measure zero.

Compute the derivative of V_{\text{dir}} along the controlled flow:

\begin{aligned}
\dot V_{\text{dir}} &= \sum_{j=1}^{N} \sin(\theta_j - \theta^{\text{ref}}) \dot\theta_j \\
&\quad + \frac{1}{2} \sum_{j,k: (k,j)\in\vec{E}} \sin(\theta_j - \theta_k) (\dot\theta_j - \dot\theta_k).
\end{aligned}

Substituting the dynamics and the control, the coupling terms partially cancel, leaving

\dot V_{\text{dir}} = -c_2 \sum_{j} |\sin(\theta_j - \theta^{\text{ref}})| - \sum_{j,k} K_{jk}^{\text{eff}} \sin^2(\theta_j - \theta_k) + \text{remainder},

where K_{jk}^{\text{eff}} > 0 due to the c_1 control term and the original coupling. The remainder involves the natural frequencies \omega_j and the bounded adversarial perturbation; its magnitude can be dominated by choosing c_2 sufficiently large relative to the frequency spread and perturbation bound. Hence \dot V_{\text{dir}} \le -W(\theta) \le 0, with W positive definite in the phase dispersion. By LaSalle’s invariance principle for non‑smooth systems, trajectories converge to the set where \sin(\theta_j - \theta^{\text{ref}}) = 0 for all j and all pairwise differences are multiples of 2\pi. However, the control term u_j prevents stabilisation at the aligned state \theta_j = \theta^{\text{ref}} because the sign function pushes the system away; the only invariant set satisfying the LaSalle condition is one where the order parameter R is below the critical threshold. A more detailed analysis of the prescribed‑time version can be found in Kuramoto et al. (2025). ∎

B.2 Dynamic Percolation Dominance (Theorem 4.3)

We present a detailed sketch based on Parshani et al. (2010). Consider a network of N nodes where at each discrete time step \Delta t, a fraction r \Delta t of randomly chosen edges are rewired (removed and reconnected randomly). Simultaneously, the defender removes a fraction r_{\text{frag}} \Delta t of edges, and the hostile AI reconnects a fraction r_{\text{reconn}} \Delta t of previously severed edges. The net rewiring rate is r = r_{\text{reconn}} - r_{\text{frag}}, assuming the two processes are independent Poisson.

The generating function formalism for the degree distribution at time t yields an equation that maps to a directed percolation problem in (d+1) dimensions, where time is the directed axis. The critical occupation probability p_c(r) satisfies

p_c(r) = p_c(0) - a\, r^{1/\nu},

where \nu = \nu_{\text{DP}} is the correlation‑length exponent of directed percolation. When r > 0, the network is more fragile: p_c(r) < p_c(0). The defender chooses r_{\text{frag}} such that r_{\text{reconn}} - r_{\text{frag}} < 0, i.e. the net rewiring rate is negative, driving p(t) downward. Even if r_{\text{frag}} is only slightly larger than r_{\text{reconn}}, the long‑time limit forces p(t) \to 0 exponentially. The probability that a giant component persists for time T decays as \exp(-c N T) in the sub‑critical regime, establishing the high‑probability guarantee. The constants are explicitly computable from the initial degree distribution. ∎

B.3 Finite‑Time Landauer Bound and Thermal Recovery Time

The standard Landauer bound Q \ge k_B T \ln 2 is the quasistatic limit. For a finite‑time erasure of duration \tau_{\text{cycle}}, Rolandi and Perarnau‑Llobet (2023) derived the geometric bound

Q \ge k_B T \ln 2 + \frac{\mathcal{C}}{\tau_{\text{cycle}}},

where \mathcal{C} = \frac{\hbar^2}{k_B T} \mathcal{L}^2 and \mathcal{L} is the thermodynamic length of the erasure protocol. The minimum cycle time is bounded below by the Planckian time \tau_{\text{Pl}} = \hbar / k_B T. Thus, the dissipation per bit erasure diverges as \tau_{\text{cycle}} \to \tau_{\text{Pl}}. For a node with cooling capacity P_{\text{cool}}, the critical erasure rate B_{\text{max}} before thermal failure is obtained from P_{\text{cool}} = B_{\text{max}} (k_B T \ln 2 + \mathcal{C}/\tau_{\text{cycle}}). The thermal recovery time after overload is

\tau_{\text{cool}} \ge \frac{C_{\text{th}} (T_{\text{crit}} - T_0)}{P_{\text{cool}}},

where C_{\text{th}} is the heat capacity of the chip and package. The pursuit–evasion solution (Theorem 6.2) exploits the fact that workload migration cannot be faster than the thermal time constants; a task cannot be safely relocated from a throttled node until it has cooled below T_0, and starting a task on a new node requires a finite spin‑up time bounded by \tau_{\text{cycle}}. The minimax solution is then a standard receding‑horizon linear program. ∎

B.4 Non‑Autonomous Lyapunov Exponent and Blind Destabilisation (Corollary 7.2)

Let the unperturbed map f be a contraction with \|Df\| \le \lambda < 1. The defender injects an i.i.d. random perturbation \eta_n \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma^2 I_d). The linearised dynamics around the fixed point \theta^* read

e_{n+1} = Df(\theta^*) e_n + \eta_n.

Although the perturbation has zero mean, its product with the state yields a stochastic contribution to the Jacobian. By Furstenberg’s theorem, the top Lyapunov exponent of the random matrix product A_n = Df + \sigma W_n (with W_n random directions) is given by

\lambda_{\max} = \log \lambda + \frac{\sigma^2}{2\lambda^2} c_d + o(\sigma^2),

for small \sigma, where c_d > 0 depends on the dimension d. The condition for \lambda_{\max} > 0 is \sigma^2 > \sigma_{\text{crit}}^2 = \frac{2\lambda^2}{c_d} |\log \lambda|. The larger the dimension or the closer \lambda is to 1, the smaller the required perturbation variance. For the deterministic persistent excitation case, a periodic perturbation cycling through the d coordinate directions with amplitude \varepsilon yields an effective Floquet exponent that can be made positive if \varepsilon > \varepsilon_{\text{crit}}. The computation of \varepsilon_{\text{crit}} uses standard averaging theory for non‑autonomous linear systems. ∎

B.5 Circular Cumulant Reduction for Finite Human Populations (Theorem 8.1)

The one‑oscillator distribution function for the human species obeys the continuity equation

\frac{\partial f}{\partial t} + \frac{\partial}{\partial \phi} (v f) = 0,

with velocity

v = \omega + \frac{1}{2\mathrm{i}}\bigl( H e^{-\mathrm{i}\phi} - \bar H e^{\mathrm{i}\phi} \bigr),

where H = K_{\text{Hh}} z^{\text{Hh}} + K_{\text{Hh,H}} z^{\text{H}} + K_{\text{Hh,B}} z^{\text{B}}. The Kuramoto–Daido order parameters are Z_n = \langle e^{\mathrm{i}n\phi} \rangle. The circular cumulants \kappa_n are defined via \ln(1 + \sum_{n\ge 1} Z_n z^n) = \sum_{n\ge 1} \kappa_n z^n. The first two cumulants relate to the order parameter and the width of the distribution: Z_1 = \kappa_1, Z_2 = \kappa_2 + \kappa_1^2. For non‑Lorentzian frequency distributions, the cumulant hierarchy does not close, but if the distribution decays sufficiently fast, the second cumulant \kappa_2 is small and the truncation error at order two is O(M^{-1/2}) for finite M due to finite‑size fluctuations.

Substituting the Fourier expansion into the continuity equation yields an infinite system of equations for Z_n, which translates into the cumulant equations. Truncating after n=2 and averaging over the frequency distribution yields the closed system (8.2). The remainder terms are bounded using concentration inequalities (Hoeffding’s inequality for bounded phases) and Gronwall’s lemma to control error propagation. The probabilistic guarantee follows from the fact that the finite‑M order parameter fluctuations are approximately Gaussian with variance \sim 1/M around the mean‑field value. For a target confidence level 1-\delta, the shield coupling K_{\text{B,Hh}}^* is chosen to satisfy the condition even in the worst‑case O(M^{-1/2}) deviation. ∎

B.6 Control Barrier Function QP Solution for Constitutional Invariance (Theorem 9.3)

The CBF condition is \dot h(\psi, u) + \lambda h(\psi) \ge 0. With h(\psi) = \gamma - |\bar\psi - \psi_{\text{ref}}|, we have

\dot h = -\operatorname{sgn}(\bar\psi - \psi_{\text{ref}}) \dot{\bar\psi}.

The average dynamics are

\dot{\bar\psi} = \bar\omega - \varepsilon (\bar\psi - \psi_{\text{ref}}) + \bar u + \bar\delta.

Thus the constraint becomes

-\operatorname{sgn}(\bar\psi - \psi_{\text{ref}}) \bigl( \bar\omega - \varepsilon (\bar\psi - \psi_{\text{ref}}) + \bar u + \bar\delta \bigr) + \lambda(\gamma - |\bar\psi - \psi_{\text{ref}}|) \ge 0.

Define \Delta = \bar\psi - \psi_{\text{ref}}. The inequality simplifies to

\bar u \ge -\bar\omega + \varepsilon \Delta - \bar\delta - \operatorname{sgn}(\Delta) \lambda(\gamma - |\Delta|).

The QP minimises \frac{1}{2} \|u\|^2 subject to \bar u = \frac{1}{P}\sum_k u_k satisfying this bound and |u_k| \le U_{\max}. This is a convex quadratic program with linear constraints, solvable in closed form via the KKT conditions. The solution is Lipschitz continuous in \psi because the constraint is affine in \bar u and the objective is strictly convex. Forward invariance follows from the standard CBF theorem (Ames et al., 2019). ∎

B.7 Self‑Stabilising Byzantine Pulse Synchronisation (Theorem 9.4)

We outline the integration of Dolev and Hoch (2007). The benevolent mesh operates in discrete rounds (pulses). Each node has a local clock; the protocol ensures that after a transient period, all non‑faulty nodes generate a pulse within a bounded skew of each other. The key parameter is the cycle length Cycle, chosen large enough to tolerate up to f < P/3 Byzantine nodes. The self‑stabilisation property ensures that even if the system starts from an arbitrary state (e.g., after a massive Byzantine attack), it converges to a synchronised state within O(f) rounds.

For fault isolation, each node broadcasts its signed phase reading at every pulse. The trusted nodes (those not yet blacklisted) compute the coordinate‑wise median of the received phases. Because the trusted set size is at least 2f+1, the median lies within the convex hull of the honest phases, and any node whose phase deviates from the median by more than 3\sigma_R is expelled. The threshold \sigma_R is estimated recursively from the variance of the trusted phases. The Daliot–Dolev compiler ensures that the expulsion protocol itself is self‑stabilising: if the trusted set is temporarily corrupted, it will recover to a consistent state. ∎

